 "timeSignature": "3/4",
 "pickupBeats": 0,
 "credits": {
  "words": "Joachim Neander (1680), translated by Catherine Winkworth (1863)",
  "tune": "German chorale \"Lobe den Herren\" (earliest known printing: Stralsund Gesangbuch, 1665)",
  "arrangement": "William Sterndale Bennett and Otto Goldschmidt (1863), adapted by Stackapella"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: Neander died 1680; Winkworth died 1878",
  "tune": "public domain: in print by 1665 (the Stralsund Gesangbuch, its earliest known printing)",
  "arrangement": "The four parts of The Chorale Book for England (public domain: published 1863; its music editors William Sterndale Bennett died 1875 and Otto Goldschmidt died 1907): the tune as printed and the bass mostly as printed, sung one note a syllable (where the page's alto, tenor or bass moves under the tune's held \"a-\" of \"cre-a-tion\", \"ra-\" of \"a-do-ra-tion\" and \"tem-ple draw\", one note of the chord is kept). Changed for 2 and 3 singers and for easy lines: the alto revoiced so the duet sings thirds and sixths (among the changes: B3, not D4, under the first \"Praise\"; B4, not G4, under \"All\", \"who\" and \"tem-\"; F#4 under the held \"a-\"; F#4 under the last \"ra-\" so 2 and 3 singers keep the third, falling to B3); the tenor revoiced where the new alto would make octaves or fifths with it; the bass changed in five ways (D3 for F#3 on the first \"the\", so the leading tone is not doubled; G3 for G2 at the end of lines 1 and 2; E3 for A3 under \"ple\"; G2 for B2 under \"Join\"; C3 for E3 under \"glad\"); three chords changed: Em7 for Em on \"to\" (the alto's D4), C for Em on \"glad\", and G/B for the page's B major on \"do-\" (its D sharp left out, so the alto is not asked for a chromatic step); the last chord has its fifth (the tenor's D3) in four parts; the tenor takes the seventh under \"near\", \"been\" and verse 1's last \"ra-\", as printed, and verse 2's \"dain-\" is a plain D chord so the tenor can hold D3 into the last chord; verse 2 sets \"sires\" to the tune's two notes on \"tem-ple\", the other parts holding a D chord under it (our underlay, as the page prints verse 2 without music); breaths written in for beginners",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "source": "The Chorale Book for England: a complete hymn-book for public and private worship, in accordance with the services and festivals of the Church of England; the hymns translated by Catherine Winkworth; the tunes compiled and edited by William Sterndale Bennett and Otto Goldschmidt (London: Longman, Green, Longman, Roberts, and Green, 1863), No. 9 (Praise and Thanksgiving), tune lxii, \"Lobe den Herren, den mächtigen König der Ehren\"; in G, printed in 3/2 (minims) and written here in 3/4 (crotchets); its first two lines under a repeat, written out here; verse 1 as printed under the music, verse 2 as printed below it (the long s written as s); read from Brigham Young University's preservation photocopy of Notre Dame University's copy at archive.org/details/choralebookforen00benn (scan pages n45 and n46; title page n10; index of tunes n411). Breaths written in, in every part, by shortening the held last note of each line: \"tion!\", \"eth,\" and \"eth;\" ending lines 1 and 2 (beats 15, 33, 78, 96) held 1.75 beats of 3; \"hear,\" and \"seen\" (39, 102), \"near,\" and \"been\" (48, 111) and verse 1's last \"tion!\" (60) held 2 beats of 3. Tune's earliest known printing: Julian, A Dictionary of Hymnology (London: John Murray, revised 1907), vol. 1, p. 683, archive.org/details/dictionaryofhymn01lond (scan page n706): it \"appeared in the Stralsund G. B., 1665\", set to \"Hast du denn Liebster dein Angesicht gäntzlich verborgen\", and was adapted by Neander; the Chorale Book's own index of tunes (n411) gives Praxis Pietatis Melica (Frankfurt, 1668). Dates, read from the scans' text layers: Neander d. Bremen, May 31, 1680 (Julian, 1892, \"Neander, Joachim\", archive.org/details/bub_gb_I-0sAAAAYAAJ); Winkworth d. Monnetier, July 1878 (Julian, 1907, vol. 2, \"Winkworth, Catherine\", archive.org/details/dictionaryofhymn02lond); Bennett d. Feb. 1, 1875 (Grove's Dictionary of Music and Musicians, 2nd edition, vol. 1, archive.org/details/grovesdictionary19121grov); Goldschmidt d. Feb. 24, 1907 (Grove's, 3rd edition, 1927, vol. 2, archive.org/details/grovesdictionary02grov_0)",
  "checked": null
 },
